 “PowerShell is a cross-platform task automation solution made up of a command-line shell, a scripting language, and a configuration management framework.”
PowerShell is a powerful tool from Microsoft designed for task automation and configuration management. It combines a command-line interface and a scripting language built on the .NET framework. Unlike older text-based command-line tools, PowerShell is object-oriented, which means it can handle complex data types and interact with system components more effectively. Initially exclusive to Windows, PowerShell has lately expanded to support macOS and Linux, making it a versatile option for IT professionals across different operating systems.
//commands of Windows PowerShell.
Get-Content: Retrieves (gets) the content of a file and displays it in the console.
Set-Location: Changes (sets) the current working directory.
Get-Command :In PowerShell, Get-Command is used to discover commands (cmdlets, functions, aliases, scripts, and applications) available in your current session or on your system.
Get-Command -CommandType "Function": lists all functions currently available in your session — including built‑in functions, functions from imported modules, and any you’ve defined yourself.
Get-Help Get-Date :Get-Help Get-Date displays the help documentation for the Get-Date cmdlet, which retrieves the current date/time or a specified date/time in PowerShell.
Get-Alias: In PowerShell, aliases are shortcuts to cmdlets, functions, or scripts. To find the owner (i.e., the command or function an alias points to), you can use Get-Alias and inspect its Definition property.
Find-Module -Name "Module_name*": find the module from the computer.
Install-Module -Name "PowerShellGet": install the module.
